Title:
ThreatIntelligenceConsumer — Consuming Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence ETW Without PPL/Driver

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
ThreatIntelligenceConsumer is a proof-of-concept that reads events from the privileged Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence ETW provider without requiring Protected Process Light (PPL) signing or a kernel driver. It abuses the protection logic around AutoLogger ETW sessions to allow a lower-privileged consumer to attach to a session that is emitting Threat-Intelligence events. The repo includes a registry file to configure an AutoLogger session at boot, and an x64 executable that attaches and prints the provider GUID for received events (full event parsing is left to the user). It targets modern Windows builds and is tested on Windows 11 24H2/25H2. This is primarily useful for red teamers and security researchers exploring ETW-based telemetry access and for defenders validating assumptions about who can consume certain ETW streams. It’s interesting because it demonstrates a user-mode path to access “security trace” telemetry typically associated with higher trust requirements.

Technical Focus:
- ETW (Event Tracing for Windows) consumer implementation
- AutoLogger session configuration via registry (boot-start ETW)
- Privileged ETW provider access controls (Threat-Intelligence / SecurityTrace)
- Logger ID discovery (WMI_LOGGER_CONTEXT / HistoricalContext)
- x64 user-mode hooking and custom assembly
- ETW event parsing concepts via TDH (Trace Data Helper)

Use Cases:
- Build a user-mode collector for Threat-Intelligence ETW events without deploying a driver
- Research/validate Windows ETW access-control boundaries around AutoLogger sessions
- Develop custom parsers for Threat-Intelligence provider events (TDH/manifest-based parsing)
- Reproduce and test the technique described in the linked OriginHQ blog post
- Defensive testing: confirm whether endpoints allow unexpected ETW consumption paths

Keywords:
ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, SecurityTrace, AutoLogger, Protected Process Light, PPL, Windows 11 24H2, Windows 11 25H2, TraceProperties, Wnode.HistoricalContext, Logger ID, WMI_LOGGER_CONTEXT, kernel debugger, TDH, Trace Data Helper, manifest-based ETW, registry AutoLogger key, x64 hooking, user-mode telemetry consumption